Title:
Cronos Sleep Obfuscation

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post introduces “Cronos,” a modern Windows sleep-obfuscation technique designed to reduce in-memory detection of implants while they are idle.  
- It targets memory scanners/EDR that can identify injected or hollowed code even when pages are non-executable or lightly obfuscated (e.g., single-byte XOR).  
- Cronos encrypts the process image with RC4 via the undocumented `SystemFunction032`, and flips page protections (RWX ↔ RW) to hinder scanning of executable regions.  
- Execution across the “encrypted/non-executable” phases is maintained using ROP, with staged transitions orchestrated via `NtContinue` context manipulation (inspired by Foliage/Ekko).  
- Waitable timers + alertable sleep (`SleepEx`) are used to reliably trigger APC-based execution of the obfuscation stages.  
- Useful primarily for red teamers, implant developers, and malware researchers evaluating or implementing sleep masking; also relevant to defenders building detections around these primitives.

Technical Focus:
- Sleep obfuscation / in-memory evasion during idle periods  
- RC4 encryption using `SystemFunction032` (advapi/crypt primitive)  
- Memory protection flipping (RWX to RW and back)  
- ROP chains to execute while code pages are non-executable/encrypted  
- `NtContinue`-based context switching to sequence stages  
- APC execution via waitable timers + alertable waits (`SleepEx`)

Use Cases:
- Implementing “sleep masking” in Windows implants/agents to reduce memory-scan exposure
- Researching reliability tradeoffs between ROP/APC/timer-based sleep obfuscation approaches
- Building detections for `SystemFunction032` usage, suspicious `NtContinue` flows, and rapid page-protection changes
- Comparing Cronos vs. Ekko/Foliage/DeathSleep techniques for operational constraints and artifacts

Keywords:
Cronos, sleep obfuscation, sleep masking, Ekko, Foliage, DeathSleep, Gargoyle, SleepyCrypt, RC4, SystemFunction032, NtContinue, ROP chain, APC, waitable timer, SleepEx, memory scanners, page protections, RWX, process image encryption, EDR evasion